"""Saint-of-the-day entries for Ora, June and July (original text). See tools/SAINTS_BRIEF.md."""
SAINTS_NEW = {
 "06-01": dict(name="Saint Justin Martyr", dates="c. 100–165 · Samaria and Rome", match=["justin"],
  history="A philosopher from Samaria who searched the schools of the Greeks for the truth and found it in Christ. He kept his philosopher’s cloak and opened a school in Rome, where he defended the faith in writing before the emperor. His account of a Sunday Mass is the earliest we have. He was beheaded for refusing to sacrifice to the gods.",
  life="Justin was born of pagan parents at Flavia Neapolis, the modern Nablus. He tried the Stoics, a follower of Aristotle, a Pythagorean and the Platonists in turn. By his own account an old man he met walking by the sea told him of the prophets, who had spoken of Christ long before, and a fire was kindled in his soul. Baptised, he taught in Rome as a Christian philosopher. His First Apology, addressed to the Emperor Antoninus Pius, describes how Christians gathered on the day of the sun, read the memoirs of the apostles, prayed, and received bread and wine which they believed to be the flesh and blood of Jesus. His Dialogue with Trypho records a long, courteous debate with a Jew. Around 165 he and six companions were tried before the prefect Rusticus, and the record of their trial survives.",
  prayerTo="Saint Justin, you sought the truth with all your mind and gave your life when you had found it. Pray for all who study and search, for teachers and for those who doubt. Help me to give a reason for the hope that is in me, with gentleness and courage, and to love the Mass you described so faithfully. Amen.",
  penance="Read a page of Scripture or of a Father of the Church slowly today, and give some time to explaining your faith kindly to someone who asks.",
  meditation="Justin held that every seed of truth in the philosophers belonged to Christ, the Word. Where have you seen a seed of His truth in someone who does not yet know Him?",
  query="Saint Justin Martyr painting"),
 "06-02": dict(name="Saints Marcellinus and Peter", dates="Died c. 304 · Rome", match=["marcellinus"],
  history="A priest and an exorcist of Rome, martyred in the persecution of Diocletian and named every day in the Roman Canon. Pope Damasus wrote that as a boy he had heard the story of their deaths from the very man who beheaded them. Constantine built a basilica over their tomb, and his mother Helena was buried beside them.",
  life="Little is certain about Marcellinus and Peter beyond their deaths, but that little is unusually well attested. In the great persecution that began under Diocletian in 303, Marcellinus, a priest, and Peter, an exorcist, were arrested in Rome. Tradition holds that in prison they converted their jailer and his family. They were led to a wood outside the city, made to clear the ground where they would be buried, and beheaded there in secret, so that Christians would not find the place. The faithful found it all the same. Pope Damasus, later in the century, set up a verse inscription at their tomb, saying he had learned of their deaths from their executioner, who had himself become a Christian. Their catacomb on the Via Labicana, with its paintings, can still be visited, and the ruins of Helena’s mausoleum stand beside it.",
  prayerTo="Saints Marcellinus and Peter, you were killed in secret and your tomb was hidden, yet the Church remembers your names at every Mass. Pray for all who suffer unseen for Christ, for prisoners and those who guard them, and for me, that I may be faithful when no one is watching. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Roman Canon slowly, or follow it attentively at Mass, and offer a prayer for those in prison and for prison officers.",
  meditation="Their killers chose a hidden place so they would be forgotten, and the hidden place became a shrine. What hidden faithfulness is God asking of you?",
  query="Saints Marcellinus and Peter painting"),
 "06-03": dict(name="Saints Charles Lwanga and Companions", dates="Died 1885–1887 · Uganda", match=["lwanga"],
  history="Twenty-two young Catholic men of the Ugandan royal court, most of them pages, killed for their faith under King Mwanga II. Charles Lwanga, their leader, baptised the catechumens among them on the night before their arrest and was burned at Namugongo on 3 June 1886. Anglican Christians died with them. Paul VI canonised them in 1964.",
  life="The Missionaries of Africa, the White Fathers, reached Buganda in 1879, and many at the court of the kabaka came to faith. King Mwanga, who came to the throne in 1884, grew to fear the new religions and to resent Christian pages who refused his sexual demands. In November 1885 he had Joseph Mukasa, the head of the pages, beheaded for rebuking him. Charles Lwanga took his place as protector of the young men. In May 1886 the king summoned the pages and demanded that the Christians separate themselves. They stepped forward, the youngest, Kizito, about fourteen. Marched to Namugongo, many were burned there together on 3 June, praying as they died. Anglican pages were among them, and the Church of Uganda honours them too. Paul VI, the first pope to visit sub-Saharan Africa, prayed at their shrine in 1969.",
  prayerTo="Saint Charles Lwanga and holy martyrs of Uganda, you were young and you chose Christ over the favour of a king. Pray for the young, especially those under pressure to do wrong, for the Church in Africa, and for unity among all who love Christ. Make me brave enough to step forward when I am asked who I am. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for young people who are pressured or exploited, and give something to a charity that protects children.",
  meditation="When the king asked the Christians to stand apart, they stepped forward. In what company do you keep quiet about Christ?",
  query="Uganda Martyrs Namugongo painting"),
 "06-05": dict(name="Saint Boniface", dates="c. 675–754 · Wessex, Germany and Frisia", match=["boniface"], efMatch="Boniface",
  history="An English monk from Wessex, born Wynfrith, who became the Apostle of Germany. He felled the sacred oak of Donar at Geismar, organised the Church across the German lands as archbishop of Mainz, and founded the abbey of Fulda. In old age he returned to Frisia as a missionary and was martyred at Dokkum with his companions.",
  life="Wynfrith was given to the monastery at Exeter as a boy and later taught at Nursling near Southampton, where he wrote a Latin grammar. In 716 he sailed for Frisia, but war closed the mission. In Rome Pope Gregory II gave him the name Boniface and a commission to preach in Germany. At Geismar in Hesse he took an axe to the oak sacred to the thunder god, and when nothing befell him many were baptised; tradition says he built a chapel from its wood. He drew monks and nuns from England to help him, among them Saint Lioba, founded dioceses and held reforming synods for the Frankish Church. Past seventy he went back to Frisia. On 5 June 754, at Dokkum, a band of raiders fell on his camp. Tradition holds that he raised a book of the Gospels over his head as the blow fell.",
  prayerTo="Saint Boniface, you left England to carry the Gospel to the peoples beyond the sea, and you never stopped being a missionary. Pray for England and Germany, for all who bring the faith to new places, and for me, that I may not be afraid to cut down the idols in my own life. Amen.",
  penance="Write to, pray for or support a missionary today, and give up one comfort as an offering for the Church in Europe.",
  meditation="He was nearly eighty when he set out for Frisia again. Is there a work for God you have decided you are too old or too tired for?",
  query="Saint Boniface painting Heinrich Maria von Hess"),
 "06-09": dict(name="Saint Ephrem", dates="c. 306–373 · Nisibis and Edessa", match=["ephrem"],
  history="A deacon of the Syrian Church whose hymns earned him the name Harp of the Holy Spirit. He wrote in Syriac, the language closest to the Aramaic of Christ, and taught the faith to his people through poetry and song, above all on Christ and on the Virgin Mary. Benedict XV named him a Doctor of the Church in 1920.",
  life="Ephrem was born at Nisibis, on the border between the Roman and Persian empires, and served its bishops as a deacon and teacher. He wrote hundreds of hymns, many for choirs of women to sing, so that sound teaching would be carried in melody against the heresies of his day. His images are rich and concrete: the pearl, the womb, the robe, the medicine of life. When Nisibis was handed to Persia in 363, Ephrem went with the Christian refugees to Edessa, where he taught in the school and lived simply. He never sought the priesthood. In the famine of 372 he organised relief for the hungry, and he died the following year. His writing shaped the worship of the Syriac Churches and later of the Greek and Latin Churches as well.",
  prayerTo="Saint Ephrem, you sang the mysteries of Christ and of His Mother, and taught the faith with beauty. Pray for the Christians of Syria and Iraq, for all who write and sing for God, and for me, that my praise may be warm and my faith true. Amen.",
  penance="Sing or listen attentively to a hymn today and pray it as a prayer. Ephrem ended his life feeding the hungry: give something to a famine appeal.",
  meditation="Ephrem compared the Word of God to a spring that no one can drink dry. Return to one passage today and drink again.",
  query="Saint Ephrem the Syrian icon"),
 "06-11": dict(name="Saint Barnabas the Apostle", dates="First century · Cyprus and Antioch", match=["barnabas"], efMatch="Barnabas",
  history="A Levite from Cyprus named Joseph, whom the apostles called Barnabas, son of encouragement. He sold a field and laid the money at their feet, vouched for Saul when the Church in Jerusalem feared him, and later brought him to Antioch. Together they made the first great missionary journey. Tradition says Barnabas was martyred in Cyprus.",
  life="The Acts of the Apostles calls Barnabas a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith. When the new convert Saul came to Jerusalem and the disciples were afraid of him, Barnabas took him to the apostles and told how he had preached boldly in Damascus. Sent to Antioch, where Gentiles were turning to the Lord, Barnabas rejoiced, then went to Tarsus to find Saul and bring him to help. It was in Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians. The Holy Spirit set the two apart, and they sailed to Cyprus and on into Asia Minor. Later they disagreed sharply over taking Barnabas’s cousin John Mark with them, and parted, Barnabas taking Mark to Cyprus. Paul later speaks warmly of both. Tradition holds that Barnabas was stoned at Salamis.",
  prayerTo="Saint Barnabas, son of encouragement, you saw good in Saul when others saw only danger, and you gave a second chance to Mark. Pray for me, that I may be generous with my goods and with my trust, and that my words may build others up in Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Encourage someone today in a specific way, by a note, a call or a word of thanks, and give alms in memory of the field Barnabas sold.",
  meditation="Without Barnabas, Saul might have been left alone. Whom could you stand beside who has been kept at a distance?",
  query="Saint Barnabas healing the sick painting Veronese"),
 "06-12": dict(name="Saint John of Sahagún", dates="1430–1479 · León and Salamanca", match=["fecundo"], efMatch="Fecundo",
  history="A Spanish priest, called John of San Facundo after his birthplace, who became an Augustinian friar and the great preacher of Salamanca. In a city torn by feuds between noble families he worked tirelessly to make peace. He was known for his long, devout celebration of Mass and for the frankness of his preaching. He is the patron of Salamanca.",
  life="John González de Castrillo was born at Sahagún in the kingdom of León and studied at the University of Salamanca. Ordained priest, he held several benefices but gave them up to live simply and preach. In 1463 he entered the Augustinian friars at Salamanca. The city was then divided by violent rivalry between two factions of nobles, and John, preaching and visiting both sides, brought many to reconciliation. He spoke plainly against injustice and the vices of the powerful, which won him enemies. He celebrated Mass slowly and with great recollection, and his brothers believed he was granted a vision of Christ at the altar. Tradition holds that he died after being poisoned by a woman whose lover he had converted. He was canonised in 1690.",
  prayerTo="Saint John of Sahagún, peacemaker of Salamanca, you went between enemies with nothing but the Gospel. Pray for families and communities divided by old quarrels, for preachers who must speak hard truths, and for me, that I may love the Mass and work for peace where I live. Amen.",
  penance="Take a first step today towards healing a quarrel, your own or one you can help with, and arrive early for Mass to pray quietly beforehand.",
  meditation="Blessed are the peacemakers. Is there a feud near you that you have simply accepted?",
  query="Saint John of Sahagun painting"),
 "06-13": dict(name="Saint Anthony of Padua", dates="1195–1231 · Lisbon and Padua", match=["padua"],
  history="A Portuguese canon who became a Franciscan, hoping for martyrdom in Morocco, and instead became the most famous preacher of his age. He taught theology to the friars at Saint Francis’s request and preached across Italy and France. He died at thirty-six and was canonised within a year. He is a Doctor of the Church and the patron of lost things.",
  life="Fernando Martins was born in Lisbon and joined the Augustinian canons, studying Scripture deeply at Coimbra. In 1220 the bodies of five Franciscans martyred in Morocco were brought there, and he resolved to follow them, taking the name Anthony. He reached Morocco but fell ill, and the ship bringing him home was driven to Sicily. Unknown and unregarded, he served in a hermitage until, at an ordination at Forlì, he was asked to preach without preparation and astonished everyone. Francis wrote to him as ‘my bishop’ and asked him to teach the brothers theology, so long as study did not quench the spirit of prayer. He preached against heresy and usury, and crowds too large for churches gathered in fields to hear him. Worn out, he died near Padua on 13 June 1231. Pius XII declared him a Doctor in 1946.",
  prayerTo="Saint Anthony, preacher of the Gospel and friend of the poor, you knew the Scriptures by heart and loved Christ in them. Help me to find what I have lost, and above all to find again any grace I have let slip. Pray for the poor, and teach me to give to them freely. Amen.",
  penance="Give alms today for the poor, in the old custom of Saint Anthony’s Bread, perhaps the cost of a meal.",
  meditation="Anthony said that actions speak louder than words, and urged preachers to let their words teach and their deeds speak. Which of your words is still waiting for its deed?",
  query="Vision of Saint Anthony of Padua painting Murillo"),
 "06-17": dict(name="Saint Gregory Barbarigo", dates="1625–1697 · Venice, Bergamo and Padua", match=["barbarigo"], efMatch="Barbarigo",
  history="A Venetian nobleman and diplomat who became a priest, cardinal and model bishop of the reform of Trent, first at Bergamo and then for over thirty years at Padua. He nursed the sick in the Roman plague, visited every parish, founded a great seminary with its own press, and longed for reunion with the Christians of the East.",
  life="Gregorio Barbarigo, of an old Venetian family, accompanied the Venetian ambassador to the peace talks at Münster that ended the Thirty Years’ War, and there met the papal envoy, the future Alexander VII, who encouraged his vocation. Ordained in 1655, he worked in Rome during the plague of 1656, caring for the sick and dying. He was made bishop of Bergamo in 1657 and cardinal in 1660, and in 1664 was moved to Padua, where he stayed until his death. Taking Saint Charles Borromeo as his model, he visited every parish, taught catechism himself, and gave away so much that he was often short of money. His seminary at Padua became famous for learning, and its press printed books in Greek, Arabic and other languages of the East, in hope of unity with separated Christians. John XXIII, who had served at Bergamo, canonised him in 1960.",
  prayerTo="Saint Gregory, faithful shepherd, you served the plague-stricken, taught the children and spent yourself for your people. Pray for our bishops and priests, for seminarians, and for the unity of Christians of East and West. Help me to serve without counting the cost. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for your bishop and for your diocesan seminarians by name if you know them, and give something to support their formation.",
  meditation="He carried the plans of a reformer and the purse of a poor man. What would change in your parish if you gave it a little more of your time?",
  query="Gregorio Barbarigo portrait painting"),
 "06-19": dict(name="Saint Romuald", dates="c. 951–1027 · Ravenna and Tuscany", match=["romuald"],
  history="A nobleman of Ravenna who became a monk in penance for a killing by his father, and spent his long life founding hermitages and monasteries across Italy. At Camaldoli in Tuscany he united the solitary life of hermits with life in community, and the Camaldolese still follow his way. He taught his monks to sit in their cell as in paradise.",
  life="Romuald’s father killed a relative in a duel over property, and Romuald, about twenty, went to the monastery of Sant’Apollinare in Classe to do forty days of penance on his behalf. He stayed and became a monk, but found the community lax and left to live under a strict hermit in the marshes near Venice. For the rest of his life he moved from place to place, reforming monasteries, gathering hermits and founding new houses, often to the annoyance of comfortable monks. Around 1012 he founded the hermitage of Camaldoli high in the Apennines, with a monastery below to receive guests and the sick. His disciple Saint Bruno of Querfurt recorded his short rule for hermits: sit in your cell as in paradise, watch your thoughts as a fisherman watches for fish, and keep to the psalms. He died at Val di Castro in 1027.",
  prayerTo="Saint Romuald, you left a life of privilege to make amends and stayed to seek God in solitude. Pray for monks and hermits, for those who do penance for others, and for me, that I may find in my own room a place of prayer, and in prayer a taste of paradise. Amen.",
  penance="Romuald began by doing penance for his father. Offer a small sacrifice today, a meal kept plain or a pleasure forgone, for someone in your family.",
  meditation="Sit in your cell as in paradise. Where is your cell, the place where you could be still before God each day?",
  query="Vision of Saint Romuald painting Andrea Sacchi"),
 "06-21": dict(name="Saint Aloysius Gonzaga", dates="1568–1591 · Lombardy and Rome", match=["aloysius"], efMatch="Aloysius",
  history="The eldest son of a marquis, trained for soldiering and the court, who gave up his inheritance to become a Jesuit. He studied in Rome under the direction of Saint Robert Bellarmine and died at twenty-three after nursing the sick in an epidemic. He is the patron of young people and of those who care for the sick.",
  life="Luigi Gonzaga was born at Castiglione delle Stiviere, heir to his father’s title, and was dressed as a little soldier almost as soon as he could walk. Serving at the courts of Florence, Mantua and Madrid, he saw their violence and vanity and turned to prayer. He received his first Communion from Saint Charles Borromeo. His father resisted his vocation for years, but in 1585 Aloysius signed his rights over to his brother and entered the Jesuit novitiate in Rome. He was not naturally gentle, and worked hard against his own stubbornness. In 1591 an epidemic struck Rome and the Jesuits opened a hospital. Aloysius begged in the streets for the sick and nursed them himself, and after carrying a dying man from the street he caught the disease. He died on 21 June 1591. Bellarmine, who had been his confessor, asked to be buried beside him.",
  prayerTo="Saint Aloysius, you gave up a title and a fortune to belong wholly to Christ, and you gave your life caring for the sick. Pray for young people, that they may be pure, brave and free. Pray for nurses and carers, and for me, that I may serve the sick as I would serve Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Visit, help or pray for someone who is sick, and give up some small luxury today as Aloysius gave up his inheritance.",
  meditation="He asked what each thing he did would matter for eternity. Ask that question once today before you choose.",
  query="Vocation of Saint Aloysius Gonzaga painting Guercino"),
 "06-22": dict(name="Saints John Fisher and Thomas More", dates="Died 1535 · Rochester and London", match=["fisher"],
  history="A bishop and a layman who were put to death under Henry VIII for refusing to accept the king as head of the Church in England. John Fisher, bishop of Rochester, was beheaded on 22 June 1535; Thomas More, the former Lord Chancellor, on 6 July. Both were canonised in 1935. More is the patron of statesmen and politicians.",
  life="John Fisher was a Yorkshireman, a Cambridge scholar and chancellor of the university, confessor to Lady Margaret Beaufort and for thirty years bishop of the poor diocese of Rochester, which he refused to leave for richer sees. Thomas More was a London lawyer, a devoted father who educated his daughters as well as his son, the author of Utopia and a friend of Erasmus. He rose to be Lord Chancellor in 1529 and resigned in 1532. Both men held that the king’s first marriage was valid and that the Pope was head of the Church, and both refused the oath demanded by the Act of Succession in 1534. They were imprisoned in the Tower. While Fisher was there, Pope Paul III made him a cardinal. Fisher went to the block on Tower Hill on 22 June 1535, More on 6 July, declaring that he died the king’s good servant, but God’s first.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Fisher and Saint Thomas More, you served your king and your country faithfully, but you would not betray your conscience or the Church. Pray for all in public life, for lawyers and judges, for bishops, and for England. Give me a conscience formed by truth, and the courage and good humour to follow it. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for those who govern, of every party, and for those who hold public office in your own area. More wore a hair shirt in secret; make a small hidden sacrifice for your country.",
  meditation="The king’s good servant, but God’s first. Where in your work do these two loyalties meet, and which comes first?",
  query="Sir Thomas More portrait Hans Holbein"),
 "06-24": dict(name="The Birth of Saint John the Baptist", dates="Solemnity · Judaea", match=["baptist"], efMatch="Baptist",
  history="Only three births are kept as feasts in the Church’s calendar: Christ’s, Our Lady’s and John the Baptist’s. John was born to Zechariah and Elizabeth in their old age, sanctified, the Church holds, when he leapt in his mother’s womb at the Visitation. His feast falls six months before Christmas, as the days begin to shorten.",
  life="Saint Luke tells how the angel Gabriel appeared to the priest Zechariah in the Temple and promised that his wife Elizabeth, long childless, would bear a son. Zechariah doubted and was struck dumb. When Mary, carrying Christ, visited her cousin, the child leapt in Elizabeth’s womb. At his circumcision the neighbours wanted to call him after his father, but Zechariah wrote, ‘His name is John,’ and at once his tongue was loosed and he sang the Benedictus, which the Church prays every morning. John grew up in the desert and came to prepare the way of the Lord, the last of the prophets and the greatest born of women. Saint Augustine saw meaning in the dates: after John’s birth the days grow shorter, after Christ’s they lengthen, for he must increase and I must decrease. The hymn for this feast, Ut queant laxis, gave us the names of the notes of the scale.",
  prayerTo="Saint John the Baptist, you leapt for joy at the coming of Christ before you were born, and you spent your life pointing to Him. Pray for me, that I may make way for Him in my heart, speak truthfully, and be glad to decrease so that He may increase. Pray for mothers and for children not yet born. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Benedictus, Luke 1:68 to 79, this morning or tonight, and make some concrete gift of help to an expectant mother or a pregnancy charity.",
  meditation="He must increase, but I must decrease. Where do you need to step aside today, so that Christ may be seen?",
  query="Birth of Saint John the Baptist painting Tintoretto"),
 "06-25": dict(name="Saint William of Vercelli", dates="1085–1142 · Vercelli and Campania", match=["william"], efMatch="William",
  history="A northern Italian who, after a pilgrimage to Compostela as a youth, became a hermit in the mountains of southern Italy. Disciples gathered round him on Monte Vergine, near Avellino, and he founded the monastery there, which became the head of a Benedictine congregation and one of the great Marian shrines of Italy.",
  life="William was born at Vercelli in Piedmont and orphaned young. Still in his teens, he set out as a pilgrim to the shrine of Saint James at Compostela, and on his return resolved to go on to Jerusalem. On the way south he was stopped, tradition holds, by robbers or by a sense that God wanted him in Italy, and he settled as a hermit on Monte Vergine. Others came to share his life, and around 1119 a church and monastery rose there. He gave his followers a strict rule of poverty and prayer, but some later wanted an easier life, and William left them in peace to found other houses in the south, among them the abbey of Goleto, where he died in 1142. King Roger II of Sicily valued his counsel. The shrine of Our Lady of Montevergine still draws great crowds of pilgrims.",
  prayerTo="Saint William, pilgrim and hermit, you set out on the road and found God waiting on a mountain. Pray for all pilgrims and for those who do not know where God is leading them. Help me to travel lightly, to pray often, and to rest at last in the Lord. Amen.",
  penance="Make a short pilgrimage on foot today, to a church or shrine near you, and pray there for someone in need.",
  meditation="He set out for Jerusalem and God stopped him halfway. Can you accept a plan of yours being changed for a better one?",
  query="Saint William of Vercelli Montevergine painting"),
 "06-26": dict(name="Saints John and Paul", dates="Fourth century · Rome", match=["john & paul"], efMatch="John & Paul",
  history="Two Roman martyrs named every day in the Roman Canon. Tradition holds they were brothers and Christian officials of the imperial household, put to death in their own house on the Caelian Hill under Julian the Apostate. A basilica was built over the house, and the rooms beneath it, with early Christian paintings, can still be seen.",
  life="The story of John and Paul comes down in a later account of their passion whose details cannot all be trusted, but their cult in Rome is very old and their names have stood in the Canon of the Mass since early times. According to that account, they had served Constantia, daughter of the Emperor Constantine, and when Julian, who abandoned the faith, came to the throne in 361, they refused to serve him or to offer incense to the gods. Given ten days to reconsider, they spent them giving away their goods to the poor. They were executed secretly in their house and buried there. In the late fourth century the senator Pammachius, a friend of Saint Jerome, built a church over the site. Excavations in the nineteenth century uncovered beneath it a Roman house with rooms painted with Christian scenes, including a figure at prayer.",
  prayerTo="Saints John and Paul, you were given ten days to deny Christ and you spent them giving to the poor. Pray for those who serve in public life, for brothers and sisters, and for households of faith. Make my home a place where Christ is loved, and my goods a gift to those in need. Amen.",
  penance="Give something you own to the poor today, an item or its value, as John and Paul did in their last days.",
  meditation="Their house became a church. What would it take for Christ to be truly at home in yours?",
  query="Saints John and Paul martyrs painting"),
 "06-28": dict(name="Saint Irenaeus", dates="c. 130–c. 202 · Smyrna and Lyons", match=["irenaeus"],
  history="A Greek from Smyrna who as a boy listened to Saint Polycarp, the disciple of Saint John, and became bishop of Lyons in Gaul. His great work Against Heresies refuted the Gnostics by appealing to Scripture and to the faith handed down in the churches from the apostles. Pope Francis named him a Doctor of the Church, the Doctor of Unity, in 2022.",
  life="Irenaeus remembered in old age how Polycarp used to describe his conversations with John and others who had seen the Lord. He went west to Lyons as a priest, and in 177 was sent to Rome with a letter to Pope Eleutherius. While he was away a fierce persecution broke out, and the aged bishop Pothinus died in prison with many of his flock. Irenaeus returned to become bishop in his place. Against the Gnostics, who claimed secret knowledge and despised the material world, he insisted on the one God who made heaven and earth, on the true flesh of Christ, and on the tradition preserved in the churches by the succession of bishops, giving the list of the bishops of Rome. When Pope Victor threatened to cut off the churches of Asia over the date of Easter, Irenaeus urged him to keep the peace. He is honoured as a martyr, though how he died is not known.",
  prayerTo="Saint Irenaeus, you received the faith from those who had known the apostles and handed it on whole. Pray for the unity of the Church, for teachers of the faith, and for me, that I may love creation as God’s gift and believe all that He has revealed. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Apostles’ Creed slowly today, pausing on each line, and do one thing to heal a division in your family or parish.",
  meditation="The glory of God is a living man, Irenaeus wrote, and the life of man is the vision of God. Where are you most fully alive?",
  query="Saint Irenaeus of Lyons painting"),
 "06-29": dict(name="Saints Peter and Paul", dates="Died c. 64–67 · Rome", match=["peter and paul", "peter & paul"], efMatch="Peter",
  history="The fisherman Christ made the rock of His Church, and the persecutor He made the apostle to the nations. Both were martyred in Rome under Nero, Peter crucified, by tradition upside down, near the Vatican Hill, and Paul beheaded on the road to Ostia. The Church has kept their feast together on this day since at least the third century.",
  life="Simon of Bethsaida left his nets at Christ’s word, confessed Him as the Son of the living God, and received the name Peter and the keys of the kingdom. He denied his Lord three times and three times was asked, ‘Do you love me?’ After Pentecost he led the Church in Jerusalem and, by ancient tradition, came at last to Rome. Saul of Tarsus, a zealous Pharisee, was thrown to the ground on the road to Damascus and rose an apostle. He crossed the Mediterranean founding churches and wrote the letters that form much of the New Testament. Writing near the end, he said, ‘I have fought the good fight, I have finished the course, I have kept the faith.’ Excavations beneath Saint Peter’s in the twentieth century uncovered the ancient shrine over Peter’s grave, and Paul lies beneath the basilica that bears his name outside the walls.",
  prayerTo="Saint Peter and Saint Paul, pillars of the Church, one who fell and was forgiven, one who persecuted and was called, pray for the Pope and for the whole Church. Give me Peter’s love and Paul’s zeal, and help me to keep the faith to the end. Amen.",
  penance="Go to Mass for this solemnity and pray for the Pope by name. Make an act of contrition for the times you have denied Christ, as Peter did.",
  meditation="Christ asked Peter only one question after the Resurrection: do you love me? Hear Him ask you the same.",
  query="Saints Peter and Paul painting El Greco"),
 "06-30": dict(name="The First Martyrs of the Holy Roman Church", dates="Died 64 · Rome", match=["first martyrs"],
  history="The unnamed Christians of Rome put to death by Nero after the great fire of July 64, which he blamed on them. The Roman historian Tacitus, no friend of the faith, records their deaths with something like pity. The day after Peter and Paul, the Church honours this great multitude who died with the apostles.",
  life="In July 64 a fire swept through Rome for nearly a week and destroyed much of the city. Rumour held that the Emperor Nero had started it, and to deflect the blame he turned on the Christians, a small and despised community. Tacitus, writing some fifty years later, says that a vast number were convicted, not so much of arson as of hatred of the human race. Some were dressed in the skins of animals and torn by dogs, some crucified, and some set alight to serve as torches in Nero’s gardens, near the Vatican Hill, while he drove among the crowds in a charioteer’s dress. Saint Clement of Rome, writing to the Corinthians at the end of the century, remembers this great multitude of the elect alongside Peter and Paul. Their names are lost, but their blood was, in Tertullian’s phrase, the seed of Christians.",
  prayerTo="Holy martyrs of Rome, you died nameless and despised, and God has written your names in heaven. Pray for Christians persecuted today, for those blamed for what they did not do, and for me, that I may not be ashamed of Christ when the world turns against Him. Amen.",
  penance="Find out about one country where Christians are persecuted now, pray for them, and give to a charity that helps them.",
  meditation="We do not know a single one of their names. Can you be content to serve God unknown?",
  query="Nero's Torches painting Henryk Siemiradzki"),
 "07-01": dict(name="The Most Precious Blood of Our Lord", dates="Instituted 1849", match=["precious blood"], efMatch="Precious Blood",
  history="A feast honouring the Blood of Christ, shed for our redemption at the circumcision, in Gethsemane, at the scourging and on the cross, and given to us in the chalice. Pius IX extended it to the whole Church in 1849. Though now joined in the newer calendar to Corpus Christi, July is still kept as the month of the Precious Blood.",
  life="Devotion to the Precious Blood runs through the Church’s history, from Saint Peter’s words that we were ransomed not with silver or gold but with the precious blood of Christ, to the mystics of the Middle Ages. In the nineteenth century it was spread by Saint Gaspar del Bufalo, who founded the Missionaries of the Precious Blood in 1815. In 1849 Pius IX, in exile at Gaeta during the revolution in Rome, was urged by one of Gaspar’s missionaries to promise the feast to the whole Church, and after his return he did so. Pius XI raised its rank in 1934, the nineteenth centenary of the Redemption, and John XXIII approved a litany of the Precious Blood in 1960. The 1969 calendar joined the feast to that of the Body and Blood of Christ, but the devotion remains, and the traditional calendar keeps it on 1 July.",
  prayerTo="Lord Jesus Christ, You shed Your Precious Blood for me in the garden, at the pillar and on the cross, and You give it to me in the chalice. Wash me clean of my sins, make me grateful for so great a price, and let Your Blood plead for the whole world before the Father. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Stations of the Cross or the Sorrowful Mysteries today, and if you can, receive Holy Communion with special thanksgiving.",
  meditation="You were bought with a price. Look at a crucifix today and ask what you are worth to Him.",
  query="Blood of the Redeemer painting Giovanni Bellini"),
 "07-03": dict(name="Saint Thomas the Apostle", dates="First century · Galilee and India", match=["thomas"],
  history="One of the Twelve, called Didymus, the Twin. He would not believe the others had seen the risen Lord until he could touch His wounds, and when Christ came again he made the clearest confession of faith in the Gospels: ‘My Lord and my God.’ Ancient tradition holds that he carried the Gospel to India and was martyred there.",
  life="Saint John’s Gospel gives Thomas a voice of his own. When Jesus resolved to go back to Judaea, where His enemies waited, it was Thomas who said, ‘Let us also go, that we may die with Him.’ At the Last Supper he asked, ‘Lord, we do not know where You are going; how can we know the way?’, and drew the answer, ‘I am the way, the truth and the life.’ Absent when the risen Christ first appeared, he refused to believe without touching the wounds. Eight days later Christ invited him to do so, and he cried out, ‘My Lord and my God.’ Christ blessed those who have not seen and yet believe. The Christians of Kerala in southern India, who call themselves Saint Thomas Christians, trace their faith to him, and tradition places his martyrdom by a spear near Chennai. His relics were later honoured at Edessa and at Ortona in Italy.",
  prayerTo="Saint Thomas, you were brave enough to go with Christ to death and honest enough to say you could not believe. Pray for all who doubt, and for the Christians of India. When my faith is weak, lead me to the wounds of Christ, so that I too may say, ‘My Lord and my God.’ Amen.",
  penance="At the elevation at Mass, or before the tabernacle, say quietly, ‘My Lord and my God.’ Pray for someone you know who struggles to believe.",
  meditation="Christ did not scold Thomas for his doubt; He showed him His wounds. What doubt could you bring honestly to Him today?",
  query="Incredulity of Saint Thomas painting Caravaggio"),
 "07-05": dict(name="Saint Anthony Mary Zaccaria", dates="1502–1539 · Cremona and Milan", match=["zaccaria"], efMatch="Zaccaria",
  history="A doctor from Cremona who became a priest and founded the Clerics Regular of Saint Paul, known as the Barnabites after their church in Milan, and an order of sisters. Devoted to Saint Paul and to the Eucharist, he worked to renew the faith of clergy and laity in a troubled age. He died at thirty-six.",
  life="Anthony Zaccaria was left fatherless as a baby and raised by a devout young mother. He studied medicine at Padua and practised as a doctor in Cremona, caring for the poor, but came to see that souls needed healing too. He taught the catechism, studied theology and was ordained in 1528. Moving to Milan, he joined a confraternity working for reform, and around 1530 founded a congregation of priests dedicated to preaching Saint Paul’s letters and calling people to conversion. It took its popular name from the church of Saint Barnabas, which they served. With Countess Ludovica Torelli he founded the Angelic Sisters of Saint Paul, and he also formed a group of married lay people. He promoted frequent Communion and the Forty Hours’ adoration of the Blessed Sacrament, and the ringing of church bells on Friday afternoons in memory of the Passion. Exhausted, he died in his mother’s arms at Cremona in 1539.",
  prayerTo="Saint Anthony Mary, physician of bodies and of souls, you loved Christ crucified and burned with the zeal of Saint Paul. Pray for doctors, for priests, and for the renewal of the Church. Help me to read the Scriptures with love and to come often to Christ in the Eucharist. Amen.",
  penance="Spend time before the Blessed Sacrament today, or read one chapter of a letter of Saint Paul slowly and prayerfully.",
  meditation="He taught that lukewarmness is the great enemy of the soul. In which part of your life have you grown lukewarm?",
  query="Saint Anthony Mary Zaccaria painting"),
 "07-06": dict(name="Saint Maria Goretti", dates="1890–1902 · Corinaldo and Nettuno", match=["goretti"],
  history="An Italian farm girl of eleven who was stabbed by a young neighbour when she resisted his attempt to assault her. Before she died the next day she forgave him and said she wanted him with her in heaven. Years later he repented and asked her mother’s forgiveness. Pius XII canonised her in 1950, before the largest crowd ever gathered for a canonisation.",
  life="Maria was born at Corinaldo, near Ancona, to a poor farming family. Seeking work, they moved to the marshes near Nettuno, south of Rome, sharing a farmhouse with the Serenelli family. Her father died of malaria in 1900, and while her mother worked the fields, Maria kept house and cared for the younger children. She had little schooling but loved her faith, and made her first Communion not long before she died. On 5 July that year Alessandro Serenelli, aged twenty, tried to force himself on her, and when she refused, saying it was a sin, he stabbed her many times. She died in hospital the next day, having forgiven him. Alessandro was sentenced to thirty years. In prison he dreamed of Maria offering him lilies, and his heart changed. On his release he asked her mother’s forgiveness and received it, and he spent the rest of his life working for Capuchin friars.",
  prayerTo="Saint Maria Goretti, you were young and poor and you chose to suffer rather than sin, and you forgave the one who killed you. Pray for children and for all who suffer violence, for the healing of those who have been harmed, and for the conversion of those who do harm. Teach me to forgive. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for victims of abuse and violence today, and support a charity that protects children. Bring before God someone you find hard to forgive.",
  meditation="She wanted the man who killed her to be with her in heaven. Is there anyone you would rather not meet there?",
  query="Saint Maria Goretti portrait"),
 "07-08": dict(name="Saint Elizabeth of Portugal", dates="1271–1336 · Aragon and Portugal", match=["elizabeth"], efMatch="Elizabeth",
  history="A princess of Aragon, named after her great-aunt Saint Elizabeth of Hungary, who was married young to King Denis of Portugal. She bore his infidelities with patience, cared for the poor and sick, and more than once made peace between her husband and their rebellious son. Widowed, she lived as a Franciscan tertiary beside the Poor Clares at Coimbra.",
  life="Elizabeth was married at about twelve to King Denis, a capable ruler but an unfaithful husband. She kept a regular life of prayer at court, founded hospitals, a shelter for abandoned women and foundling homes, and brought up the king’s children by other women with kindness. When her son Afonso rose in arms against his father, she worked tirelessly for reconciliation, and in 1323, tradition says, rode out between the two armies to stop the battle. After Denis died in 1325 she made a pilgrimage to Compostela, then lived simply in a house beside the convent of Poor Clares she had founded at Coimbra. In 1336, though old and ill, she travelled in summer heat to make peace between her son, now king, and the king of Castile. She died at Estremoz soon after and was canonised in 1625. Portugal remembers her as the Holy Queen.",
  prayerTo="Saint Elizabeth, queen and peacemaker, you answered unfaithfulness with patience and war with reconciliation. Pray for marriages under strain, for families at odds, and for all who rule. Give me the courage to step between those who quarrel, and the humility to begin with myself. Amen.",
  penance="Do something kind today for a family member you find difficult, and pray for a marriage you know is struggling.",
  meditation="She rode out between her husband and her son. Where could you be a bridge rather than take a side?",
  query="Saint Elizabeth of Portugal painting Zurbarán"),
 "07-09": dict(name="Saint Augustine Zhao Rong and Companions", dates="1648–1930 · China", match=["zhao"],
  history="One hundred and twenty martyrs who died in China over nearly three centuries: eighty-seven Chinese Catholics, priests, catechists, laymen, women and children, and thirty-three missionaries from Europe. Augustine Zhao Rong, a soldier who became a priest, died in prison in 1815. Saint John Paul II canonised them together in 2000.",
  life="Christianity reached China more than once, and from the seventeenth century missionaries built communities that endured repeated persecution. The first of these martyrs, the Dominican Francis Fernández de Capillas, was killed in 1648. Augustine Zhao Rong was a soldier who escorted the French missionary bishop Gabriel Taurin Dufresse to his martyrdom in 1815. Moved by the bishop’s patience, he asked for baptism, was ordained priest, and was himself arrested, tortured and died in prison. Many of the others died in the violence of 1900, among them whole families, catechists, seminarians, and girls in their teens, some of whom were offered their lives if they would renounce Christ and refused. The last died in 1930. The Church honours them not for any cause of nation or politics but because they would not deny Christ, and many died praying for those who killed them.",
  prayerTo="Holy martyrs of China, you were soldiers and farmers, priests and children, and you would not deny Christ. Pray for the Catholics of China and for all their people, that the Gospel may be heard there in freedom. Pray for me, that I may love Christ more than my life. Amen.",
  penance="Pray a decade of the Rosary today for the Church in China, and offer a small fast, perhaps a simple meal, for persecuted Christians everywhere.",
  meditation="A soldier watched a bishop die and asked to be baptised. Who is watching how you carry your cross?",
  query="Martyrs of China painting"),
 "07-10": dict(name="The Seven Holy Brothers, and Saints Rufina and Secunda", dates="Second and third centuries · Rome", match=["seven holy brothers"], efMatch="Seven",
  history="Two groups of early Roman martyrs kept together on this day. The seven brothers, by tradition sons of Saint Felicity, died under Antoninus Pius in the second century; Rufina and Secunda, two sisters who had vowed themselves to God, died under Valerian in the third. The brothers were already honoured on 10 July in the Roman calendar of 354.",
  life="The old Roman list of the burials of martyrs, compiled in 354, records on this day seven martyrs buried in four different cemeteries around the city: Felix and Philip, Martial, Vitalis and Alexander, Silvanus, and Januarius. A later account made them the seven sons of Saint Felicity, a widow who encouraged each of them to die bravely before she died herself, like the mother in the Book of Maccabees. Whatever their family ties, their cult is ancient and well founded. Rufina and Secunda, according to their tradition, were sisters of a Christian family in Rome who refused marriage in order to belong to Christ, and who were arrested when they would not deny Him. They were put to death on the Via Cornelia, outside the city, around 257. A bishopric near Rome later bore Rufina’s name.",
  prayerTo="Holy brothers and holy sisters, martyrs of Rome, you encouraged one another to be faithful to the end. Pray for brothers and sisters in every family, for mothers who hand on the faith, and for all consecrated virgins. Help my family to strengthen one another in Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for your own brothers and sisters, or those who have been like them to you, and contact one of them.",
  meditation="Seven brothers, two sisters, one Lord. How do you help those closest to you to be faithful?",
  query="Saint Felicity and her seven sons painting"),
 "07-11": dict(name="Saint Benedict", dates="c. 480–547 · Nursia, Subiaco and Monte Cassino", match=["benedict"],
  history="The father of monks in the West. Leaving his studies in Rome, he lived as a hermit at Subiaco, then founded the monastery of Monte Cassino and wrote the Rule that has guided monks and nuns ever since. His sons and daughters carried learning and prayer across Europe, and Paul VI named him patron of Europe in 1964.",
  life="All we know of Benedict comes from the Dialogues of Pope Saint Gregory the Great, written about fifty years after his death. Born at Nursia in Umbria, he was sent to study in Rome, but the city’s vice drove him away, and he lived for three years in a cave at Subiaco. Monks nearby asked him to be their abbot, and when his strictness displeased them, they tried to poison him. He went on to found twelve small monasteries around Subiaco, and around 529 moved to Monte Cassino, where he built his great abbey on the site of a pagan temple. There he wrote his Rule, which opens, ‘Listen, my son,’ and orders the day around prayer, work and holy reading with moderation and good sense. Its heart is to prefer nothing to the love of Christ. His twin sister Saint Scholastica led nuns nearby. He died standing in the chapel, supported by his monks.",
  prayerTo="Saint Benedict, father of monks, you taught that nothing is to be preferred to the love of Christ. Pray for Europe, for all monks and nuns, and for me, that I may listen with the ear of my heart, keep a rhythm of prayer and work, and receive every guest as Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Keep one of the hours of prayer today, Morning or Evening Prayer, and welcome someone as Benedict asked: as if they were Christ.",
  meditation="Listen with the ear of your heart, the Rule begins. When did you last truly listen, to God or to anyone?",
  query="Life of Saint Benedict fresco Sodoma Monte Oliveto"),
 "07-12": dict(name="Saint John Gualbert", dates="c. 995–1073 · Florence and Vallombrosa", match=["gualbert"], efMatch="Gualbert",
  history="A young Florentine nobleman who, on Good Friday, met the man who had killed his brother and, when the man begged for mercy with his arms outstretched like a cross, spared him. Tradition holds that the crucifix in the church where he then prayed bowed its head to him. He became a monk and founded the abbey of Vallombrosa.",
  life="John Gualbert was bound by the code of his class to avenge his brother’s murder. Riding into Florence with armed men on Good Friday, he came upon the killer in a narrow way where neither could pass. The man fell to his knees and stretched out his arms, and John, remembering Christ who forgave on that day, put up his sword and embraced him. He went into the church of San Miniato to pray, and the crucifix, as tradition tells it, bowed to him. He became a Benedictine monk there, but left when a new abbot bought his office. After a time at Camaldoli he founded his own community in the forested valley of Vallombrosa, east of Florence, under a strict Benedictine rule. He fought simony fearlessly and cared for the poor in famine. He died in 1073 and was canonised in 1193. Pius XII named him patron of foresters.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Gualbert, you had every right by the world’s standards to take revenge, and on Good Friday you chose mercy. Pray for all who have been gravely wronged, for those who long for revenge, and for me, that I may forgive as Christ forgave me, from the cross. Amen.",
  penance="Pray for someone who has hurt you, by name, and do them some small good today if you can. Spend a few minutes before a crucifix.",
  meditation="The man who killed his brother held out his arms like a cross, and John saw Christ in him. Whose outstretched arms have you ignored?",
  query="The Merciful Knight Burne-Jones"),
 "07-13": dict(name="Saint Henry", dates="973–1024 · Bavaria and Bamberg", match=["henry"],
  history="Duke of Bavaria, king of Germany and from 1014 Holy Roman Emperor, Henry II governed as a Christian ruler, supported the reform of monasteries and founded the diocese of Bamberg, where he and his wife Saint Cunigunde are buried. He is the only German king to be canonised and is a patron of Benedictine oblates.",
  life="Henry was educated by Saint Wolfgang, bishop of Regensburg, and inherited the duchy of Bavaria. Chosen king in 1002, he spent much of his reign holding together a fractious realm, and his wars were not all glorious, but he saw his office as a service of God and of the Church. He founded the diocese of Bamberg in 1007 and built its cathedral, endowed monasteries and supported the reforming monks of Cluny and Lorraine. Pope Benedict VIII crowned him emperor in Rome in 1014, and at Henry’s request the Creed began to be sung at Mass in Rome, as it already was north of the Alps. He and Cunigunde had no children, and a later tradition held that they lived as brother and sister. He died in 1024 and was canonised in 1146.",
  prayerTo="Saint Henry, you held great power and tried to use it for God and for your people. Pray for all who govern, for husbands and wives, and for those who serve the Church as lay people. Help me to do my daily work as a service to Christ the King. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for those who hold power in your country and abroad, and offer your day’s work, whatever it is, explicitly to God.",
  meditation="An emperor can be a saint, and so can you, in whatever station God has placed you. What would holiness look like in your job today?",
  query="Saint Henry II and Cunigunde painting"),
 "07-14": dict(name="Saint Camillus de Lellis", dates="1550–1614 · Abruzzo and Rome", match=["camillus"],
  history="A soldier and gambler who lost everything, was converted while labouring for Capuchin friars, and gave the rest of his life to the sick. He founded the Ministers of the Sick, whose red cross later became a familiar sign of care for the wounded. He is the patron of the sick, of nurses and of hospitals.",
  life="Camillus was born at Bucchianico in the Abruzzo, a large, quick-tempered young man who followed his father into soldiering. He gambled away his money and even his shirt. In 1575, working as a labourer at a Capuchin friary in Manfredonia, he was moved by a friar’s words and resolved to change his life. He tried to become a Capuchin but an ulcer on his leg, which troubled him all his life, kept him out. He went instead to the hospital of San Giacomo in Rome as a patient and then as a nurse, and rose to run it. Appalled by the neglect of the sick, he gathered men who would care for them as a mother cares for her only child. With Saint Philip Neri as his confessor he was ordained in 1584. His congregation nursed the plague-stricken and served on the battlefield. He died in Rome in 1614.",
  prayerTo="Saint Camillus, you knew weakness and sin and you found Christ in the sick. Pray for the sick and dying, for nurses, doctors and carers, and for hospitals. Give me a heart that serves the suffering as a mother serves her only child, and that does not give up on itself. Amen.",
  penance="Visit someone who is sick or lonely, or do a practical service for a carer who is worn out. If you have a habit you cannot break, offer it to God today.",
  meditation="He told his brothers to put more heart into their hands. Where could you put more heart into what your hands do?",
  query="Saint Camillus de Lellis painting Pierre Subleyras"),
 "07-15": dict(name="Saint Bonaventure", dates="c. 1217–1274 · Bagnoregio, Paris and Lyons", match=["bonaventure"],
  history="A Franciscan theologian who taught at Paris alongside Saint Thomas Aquinas, then led the Franciscan order for seventeen years as its minister general, so wisely that he is called its second founder. He wrote the official life of Saint Francis and The Journey of the Mind into God. He is the Seraphic Doctor of the Church.",
  life="Giovanni di Fidanza was born at Bagnoregio near Viterbo. Tradition holds that as a child he was cured of a grave illness through the prayers of Saint Francis. He studied at Paris, joined the Franciscans, and became a master of theology at the same time as Aquinas. In 1257 he was elected minister general of an order divided between those who wanted strict poverty and those who wanted to adapt. He held it together with firmness and charity, gave it constitutions, and wrote the Major Life of Francis. His mystical works trace how all creation, rightly seen, leads the soul upward to God. Gregory X made him cardinal bishop of Albano in 1273, and the story is told that when the envoys came with his red hat he was washing dishes and asked them to hang it on a tree. He died during the Council of Lyons in 1274, which he had helped to prepare.",
  prayerTo="Saint Bonaventure, you studied deeply and loved more deeply still, and you saw the footprints of God in all creation. Pray for teachers and students of theology, for the Franciscan family, and for me, that my learning may lead me to prayer and my prayer to love. Amen.",
  penance="Take a walk today and look at created things as Bonaventure did, as traces of God, and finish whatever humble chore you would rather avoid.",
  meditation="He asked his readers to look at creation as a ladder to God. What in nature has drawn you towards Him?",
  query="Saint Bonaventure painting Zurbarán"),
 "07-16": dict(name="Our Lady of Mount Carmel", dates="Carmelite feast, extended 1726", match=["carmel"],
  history="Mount Carmel in the Holy Land is where the prophet Elijah called down fire from heaven. In the twelfth century hermits settled there in honour of Our Lady and became the Carmelite order. This feast, their patronal feast, spread to the whole Church, and with it the Brown Scapular, a sign of consecration to Mary.",
  life="The first Carmelites were Latin hermits who gathered by the spring of Elijah on Mount Carmel and received a rule from Saint Albert, patriarch of Jerusalem, in the early thirteenth century. As the Crusader kingdoms fell they moved to Europe, including England, where they became friars. A cherished tradition holds that the English Carmelite Saint Simon Stock, praying for his struggling order, received a vision of Our Lady holding out the scapular, the part of the habit worn over the shoulders, as a sign of her protection. The vision is a matter of pious tradition, but the Church has long approved the small Brown Scapular as a sacramental, worn as a pledge of devotion to Mary and of a Christian life. The feast was kept in England in the fourteenth century and extended to the whole Church in 1726. The Carmelite friars returned to their old priory at Aylesford in Kent in 1949.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, Our Lady of Mount Carmel, flower of Carmel and splendour of heaven, clothe me in your protection as a mother clothes her child. Help me to live the faith I profess, to pray as the prophets prayed, and to die in the friendship of your Son. Amen.",
  penance="If you wear the Brown Scapular, renew your devotion; if not, ask a priest to enrol you. Spend ten minutes in silent prayer as the hermits of Carmel did.",
  meditation="Elijah heard God not in the wind or the earthquake or the fire, but in a still small voice. Make space today to hear it.",
  query="Madonna del Carmelo painting Tiepolo"),
 "07-20": dict(name="Saint Apollinaris", dates="Early centuries · Ravenna", match=["apollinaris"],
  history="The first bishop of Ravenna, honoured there as a martyr since very early times. Tradition, recorded centuries later, made him a disciple sent from Antioch by Saint Peter. The great basilica of Sant’Apollinare in Classe, consecrated in 549, keeps his memory, and its apse mosaic shows him in prayer among his flock, pictured as sheep.",
  life="Almost nothing certain is known of Apollinaris except that he founded the Church in Ravenna and that his people honoured him as their first bishop and a witness for Christ. Saint Peter Chrysologus, preaching in Ravenna in the fifth century, spoke of him as a bishop who had suffered much for the faith, and the city built him two great churches. A later account of his life, written to support Ravenna’s dignity, tells that Peter sent him from Antioch, that he worked wonders, was repeatedly beaten and exiled, and returned each time to his flock until he died of his wounds. Whatever the details, his memory has been kept with great love. In the apse at Classe, above a field of trees, flowers and birds, he stands with his arms raised in prayer beneath a jewelled cross, the shepherd of a flock of twelve white sheep.",
  prayerTo="Saint Apollinaris, first shepherd of Ravenna, you suffered for your flock and would not abandon them. Pray for bishops and for all who lead the Church, for those who are exiled or driven out, and for me, that I may stand among God’s people with my hands raised in prayer. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for your bishop and for the first bishops of your own diocese. Pray with your hands raised for a few moments, as Apollinaris is shown.",
  meditation="He is remembered not by his words but by his prayer among his people. How do others see you pray?",
  query="Sant'Apollinare in Classe apse mosaic"),
 "07-21": dict(name="Saint Lawrence of Brindisi", dates="1559–1619 · Brindisi, Central Europe and Lisbon", match=["brindisi"], efMatch="Brindisi",
  history="A Capuchin friar of extraordinary gifts: a master of many languages, a tireless preacher, minister general of his order, and a peacemaker trusted by princes. He was chaplain to the imperial army that fought the Turks in Hungary in 1601. John XXIII named him a Doctor of the Church in 1959, the Apostolic Doctor.",
  life="Giulio Cesare Russo was born at Brindisi in Apulia and was orphaned young. He entered the Capuchins at Verona in 1575 and took the name Lawrence. He studied at Padua and mastered Hebrew, Greek, German, Bohemian and other languages, and knew much of the Bible by heart in its original tongues. Sent to preach in Rome and then across Italy, Austria, Bohemia and Germany, he founded friaries and strengthened Catholics in lands divided by the Reformation. In 1601, as chaplain to the imperial forces at Székesfehérvár in Hungary, he rode before the troops holding a crucifix. Elected minister general in 1602, he visited his friars on foot across Europe. He served as an envoy for peace between Catholic princes, and in 1619 travelled to Lisbon to plead with the king of Spain on behalf of the people of Naples against their viceroy. He died there on his birthday, 22 July.",
  prayerTo="Saint Lawrence, you used every gift God gave you for the Gospel and for peace. Pray for preachers and scholars, for those who work for peace between nations, and for me, that I may love the Word of God, learn it by heart, and speak up for those who are oppressed. Amen.",
  penance="Learn one short passage of Scripture by heart today, and do something practical for someone treated unjustly.",
  meditation="He crossed Europe on foot to plead for the poor of Naples. Whose cause could you take up, even in a small way?",
  query="Saint Lawrence of Brindisi painting"),
 "07-22": dict(name="Saint Mary Magdalene", dates="First century · Magdala", match=["magdalene"], efMatch="Magdalene",
  history="A woman of Magdala in Galilee from whom Christ cast out seven devils. She followed Him with the other women, stood by the cross, and was the first to see Him risen, sent by Him to tell the apostles. The Church calls her the apostle to the apostles, and in 2016 her memorial was raised to a feast.",
  life="Saint Luke names Mary Magdalene first among the women who travelled with Jesus and provided for Him out of their means. When the apostles fled, she stood at the cross with the Mother of Jesus, and she watched where He was laid. Early on the first day of the week she came to the tomb and found it empty. Weeping, she did not know the risen Christ until He spoke her name, and she answered, ‘Rabboni.’ He told her not to cling to Him but to go to His brethren, and she went and announced, ‘I have seen the Lord.’ Western tradition, following Saint Gregory the Great, long identified her with the woman who wept over Christ’s feet and with Mary of Bethany, and so she became the great model of the repentant sinner. The calendar now distinguishes them, but her love and her tears for the Lord are certain.",
  prayerTo="Saint Mary Magdalene, you were set free by Christ and loved Him to the end, at the cross and at the empty tomb. Pray for all who are bound by sin or by evil, for those who weep, and for me, that I may know the Lord when He calls my name and run to tell others. Amen.",
  penance="Go to Confession if you can, or make a careful examination of conscience tonight. Tell one person today something Christ has done for you.",
  meditation="She did not recognise Him until He said her name. Be still today and let Him say yours.",
  query="Noli me tangere fresco Fra Angelico"),
 "07-24": dict(name="Saint Charbel Makhlouf", dates="1828–1898 · Lebanon", match=["charbel"],
  history="A Maronite monk and priest of Lebanon who spent his last twenty-three years as a hermit, in prayer, fasting and manual work, celebrating Mass with great devotion. After his death light was reported around his grave and his body was found incorrupt. Thousands of healings have been attributed to him. Paul VI canonised him in 1977.",
  life="Youssef Antoun Makhlouf was born in the mountain village of Bekaa Kafra, in northern Lebanon, and lost his father when he was three. Raised by his mother and devout uncles, two of whom were hermits, he left home at twenty-three to enter the Lebanese Maronite Order, taking the name Charbel after an early martyr. He was ordained priest in 1859 and lived at the monastery of Saint Maron at Annaya for sixteen years. In 1875 he was allowed to withdraw to the hermitage of Saints Peter and Paul above the monastery, where he prayed, worked the land, ate once a day and spoke little. Each day he offered Mass with deep recollection. In December 1898 he suffered a stroke while celebrating Mass and died on Christmas Eve. His tomb at Annaya draws pilgrims of every faith, Christian and Muslim alike.",
  prayerTo="Saint Charbel, hermit of Lebanon, you left everything to live hidden with God, and He has made your hiddenness a light to many. Pray for Lebanon and the Christians of the Middle East, for the sick who come to you, and for me, that I may find God in silence and in the Mass. Amen.",
  penance="Eat simply today, keeping to one main meal if your health allows, and pray for peace in Lebanon and the Middle East.",
  meditation="He hardly spoke, and yet he still draws the world to God. What would God say through your silence?",
  query="Saint Charbel Makhlouf"),
 "07-25": dict(name="Saint James the Apostle", dates="Died c. 44 · Galilee, Jerusalem and Compostela", match=["james"],
  history="James, son of Zebedee and brother of Saint John, was a fisherman whom Christ called from his nets. With Peter and John he saw the Transfiguration and the agony in the garden. He was the first of the Twelve to die for Christ, beheaded by Herod Agrippa in Jerusalem. Tradition links him with Spain and his shrine at Compostela.",
  life="Christ found James and John mending nets in their father’s boat, and they left him with the hired men and followed. He called the brothers Boanerges, sons of thunder, perhaps for their temper: they once wanted to call down fire on a Samaritan village that would not receive Him. Their mother asked that they might sit at His right and left in His kingdom, and Christ asked whether they could drink His cup. James said they could, and he did. Around the year 44 Herod Agrippa I had him put to the sword, the only death of an apostle recorded in the New Testament. A tradition holds that James had preached in Spain and that his body was brought there. In the ninth century a tomb believed to be his was found in Galicia, and Santiago de Compostela became, with Rome and Jerusalem, one of the great pilgrimages of Christendom. Pilgrims still walk the Camino, with the scallop shell as their sign.",
  prayerTo="Saint James, son of thunder, you promised to drink the cup of Christ and you kept your word with your life. Pray for pilgrims on every road, for Spain, and for me, that my zeal may become charity, that I may walk faithfully with Christ, and that I may reach the end of the way. Amen.",
  penance="Walk somewhere today you would usually drive, offering it as a small pilgrimage, and pray for all who are on the Camino.",
  meditation="Can you drink the cup that I drink? James answered yes before he knew what it would cost. What is your answer today?",
  query="Saint James the Greater painting Guido Reni"),
 "07-26": dict(name="Saints Joachim and Anne", dates="First century BC · Parents of the Blessed Virgin", match=["joachim", "anne"], efMatch="Anne",
  history="The parents of the Virgin Mary and grandparents of Jesus. Their names are not in Scripture but come from an ancient tradition recorded in the second century. They are honoured as a holy couple who waited long for a child and offered her to God. They are patrons of grandparents, and the Church keeps a World Day for Grandparents and the Elderly near their feast.",
  life="The second-century writing known as the Protoevangelium of James tells that Joachim, a wealthy and devout man, and his wife Anne were childless into old age, and that Joachim was turned away from offering his gift in the Temple because of it. He went into the wilderness to fast and pray, and Anne prayed at home, and an angel told each of them that they would have a child. They met at the Golden Gate of Jerusalem in joy, and Mary was born to them. The story is not Scripture, and the Church does not vouch for its details, but it carries a true instinct: that Mary was prepared for by a faithful family. Devotion to Anne grew in the East from the sixth century and spread through the West in the Middle Ages, being especially strong in medieval England. Great shrines to her stand at Auray in Brittany and Sainte-Anne-de-Beaupré in Canada.",
  prayerTo="Saint Joachim and Saint Anne, you waited in faith and raised the Mother of God in a home of prayer. Pray for grandparents and the elderly, for couples who long for children, and for every family. Help me to hand on the faith to those who come after me. Amen.",
  penance="Visit, call or write to a grandparent or an elderly person today. If your grandparents have died, pray for them by name.",
  meditation="The grace given to Mary passed through an ordinary home. What are you handing on to the next generation?",
  query="Meeting at the Golden Gate fresco Giotto"),
 "07-28": dict(name="Saints Nazarius and Celsus, Victor and Innocent", dates="Early centuries · Milan and Rome", match=["nazarius"], efMatch="Nazarius",
  history="The traditional calendar joins two martyrs of Milan and two popes on this day. Saint Ambrose found the body of Nazarius in a garden outside Milan in 395, and with it that of the boy Celsus. Victor I, an African, was pope at the end of the second century. Innocent I led the Church in Rome when the city was sacked by the Goths in 410.",
  life="Paulinus, the biographer of Saint Ambrose, tells how in 395 the bishop uncovered the body of the martyr Nazarius in a garden outside the walls of Milan and carried it to the new church of the Apostles, where he is still honoured. Nearby lay Celsus, whom tradition makes a boy disciple martyred with him; when or how they died is unknown. Victor I, pope around 189 to 199, is said to have been the first Latin-speaking bishop of Rome and tried to make all the churches keep Easter on a Sunday, threatening to cut off the churches of Asia until Saint Irenaeus urged him to patience. He is honoured as a martyr by tradition. Innocent I, pope from 401 to 417, defended Saint John Chrysostom when he was exiled, confirmed the African bishops’ condemnation of Pelagius, and laboured for Rome through the barbarian invasions, being at Ravenna seeking peace when Alaric sacked the city.",
  prayerTo="Holy martyrs Nazarius and Celsus, holy popes Victor and Innocent, you served Christ in times of persecution, division and collapse. Pray for the Church in every hard season, for the Pope and the bishops, and for me, that I may stand firm when the world around me falls apart. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for the Pope, and for a city or country suffering war or collapse. Give something to help refugees.",
  meditation="Innocent watched Rome fall and kept the Church steady. When everything feels uncertain, what in your faith stays fixed?",
  query="Saints Nazarius and Celsus painting"),
 "07-29": dict(name="Saint Martha", dates="First century · Bethany", match=["martha"], efMatch="Martha",
  history="The sister of Mary and Lazarus of Bethany, whose house was a home to Christ. Saint Luke tells of her busy with serving while Mary sat at His feet; Saint John records her great confession of faith at the tomb of Lazarus. Since 2021 the Church keeps the three siblings together on this day. She is the patron of cooks and of all who serve.",
  life="Martha appears in the Gospels as practical, warm and direct. When Christ came to the house at Bethany, she was distracted with much serving and asked Him to tell her sister to help, and He answered gently, ‘Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things; one thing is needful.’ When Lazarus died, it was Martha who went out to meet Jesus and said, ‘Lord, if You had been here, my brother would not have died.’ He told her, ‘I am the resurrection and the life,’ and she answered, ‘Yes, Lord, I believe that You are the Christ, the Son of God.’ At a supper six days before the Passover, after Lazarus had been raised, Martha served again. A medieval tradition says she later went to Provence and tamed a dragon at Tarascon, where she is honoured to this day.",
  prayerTo="Saint Martha, you welcomed Christ into your home and served Him with your hands, and you believed in Him when your brother lay in the tomb. Pray for all who cook, clean and care for others, and for me, that I may not be anxious about many things, but choose the one thing needful. Amen.",
  penance="Cook a meal or do a household task for someone today as an act of love, and then take ten quiet minutes, like Mary, at the Lord’s feet.",
  meditation="One thing is needful. What are the many things that are crowding it out today?",
  query="Christ in the House of Martha and Mary painting Vermeer"),
 "07-30": dict(name="Saint Peter Chrysologus", dates="c. 380–450 · Imola and Ravenna", match=["chrysologus"],
  history="Bishop of Ravenna when it was the capital of the Western Empire, Peter was later called Chrysologus, golden word, for his preaching. Many of his sermons survive: short, vivid and full of love for the Incarnation and the Eucharist. He urged loyalty to the Bishop of Rome in matters of faith. He was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1729.",
  life="Peter was born at Imola and served its bishop as a deacon before being made bishop of Ravenna around 433. The imperial court was then at Ravenna, and the Empress Galla Placidia supported his building of churches and care for the city. His people loved his preaching, which was brief because, he said, he did not want to tire them. Around 180 of his sermons survive, on the Gospels, the Creed, the Lord’s Prayer and the mysteries of Christ’s birth, and he often turns to the Eucharist as the bread come down from heaven. When the monk Eutyches, condemned for teaching that Christ had only one nature, appealed to him, Peter replied that he should listen to the Bishop of Rome, through whom Saint Peter still teaches the faith to those who seek it. He died at Imola around 450. Benedict XIII named him a Doctor of the Church in 1729.",
  prayerTo="Saint Peter Chrysologus, golden-tongued preacher, you spoke briefly and lovingly of the mysteries of Christ. Pray for all who preach, that they may speak with clarity and warmth, and for me, that I may hunger for the Word and for the Bread of life. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Our Father very slowly today, one phrase at a time, and give the price of a loaf of bread to a food bank.",
  meditation="He kept his sermons short for love of his hearers. How could you say what matters more simply today?",
  query="Saint Peter Chrysologus painting"),
 "07-31": dict(name="Saint Ignatius of Loyola", dates="1491–1556 · Loyola, Paris and Rome", match=["loyola"], efMatch="Loyola",
  history="A Basque soldier whose leg was shattered by a cannonball at Pamplona, and who, reading the life of Christ during his long recovery, gave himself to God. From his own experience he wrote the Spiritual Exercises, and with companions from the University of Paris he founded the Society of Jesus. He governed it from Rome until his death.",
  life="Íñigo López de Loyola was a courtier and soldier who dreamed of glory and of noble ladies. Wounded at Pamplona in 1521, he lay for months at Loyola with nothing to read but a life of Christ and lives of the saints. He noticed that daydreams of worldly fame left him empty, while thoughts of following Christ left him at peace, and from this he learned to discern spirits. At Montserrat he hung his sword before the image of Our Lady, and at Manresa he spent a year in prayer and penance, drafting the Exercises. He went back to school with boys, and at Paris won over companions, among them Francis Xavier and Peter Faber. In 1534 they made vows together at Montmartre, and in 1540 Paul III approved the Society of Jesus. Ignatius spent his last years in Rome writing its constitutions and sending his sons across the world. He died on 31 July 1556.",
  prayerTo="Saint Ignatius, you learned to find God in all things and to seek His greater glory in everything. Pray for the Society of Jesus, for all who make or give the Exercises, and for me, that I may notice how God moves in my heart, choose what leads me to Him, and give Him my whole self. Amen.",
  penance="Make the Examen tonight: review the day with God, give thanks, notice where you drew near to Him or away from Him, and ask pardon and help for tomorrow.",
  meditation="For the greater glory of God: Ignatius asked which of two good things would give God more glory. Ask that of one choice you make today.",
  query="Saint Ignatius of Loyola painting Rubens"),
}
